From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Subject: [PATCH] battery: report voltage, current and temperature in the units
 they claim

batteryd publishes these as "voltage_mV", "current_mA" and "temperature_C",
and every consumer treats them that way. They were the raw sysfs readings.
The power_supply class exports voltage_now and current_now in microvolts and
microamps and temp in tenths of a degree, so a 3.84 V cell drawing 212 mA at
29 degrees was reported as 3843000 mV, -212000 mA and 290 C.

The inconsistency is inside this one file: battery_coulomb() and
battery_full40() already divide their microamp-hour readings down to mAh,
each with a comment saying so. These three simply never got the same
treatment.

It is not only a display problem. The CTIA limits a few lines above -
CHARGE_MIN_TEMPERATURE_C 0, CHARGE_MAX_TEMPERATURE_C 57,
BATTERY_MAX_TEMPERATURE_C 60 - are compared against this value in
charging_logic.c. With temp in tenths, an ordinary 30 degree battery reads
300 and sits permanently above all three, so the shutdown check was always
true and the charge window meant nothing.

The tests asked the question and never answered it: "Should this be in mV or
uV? Device returns uV but emulator returns mV". The kernel's power_supply
ABI settles it - microvolts, microamps, tenths of a degree - and a driver
that reports otherwise is out of spec. Guessing by magnitude is not an
option: it would work for voltage, where microvolts and millivolts cannot be
confused, and not for current or temperature, where 2000 and 45 are
plausible readings either way. Better to be consistent with the ABI and with
the two capacity readers than to guess for one of the three.

Temperature is rounded rather than truncated so a battery below freezing
does not come out warmer than it is; current truncates toward zero, which is
what dropping sub-milliamp precision should do.

Tests updated for the new values, with cases added for rounding at and below
zero and either side of the 60 degree shutdown limit. battery_avg_current
delegates to battery_current and needed the same change - the suite caught
that. All three suites pass: test_dev_battery 15, test_batterylib 10,
test_utils 4.

Upstream-Status: Pending [carried until the fix is merged in the
webOS-ports nyx-modules fork and SRCREV moves]

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01RaHwdzWteqUYGfRe1aJwhS
---
 src/battery/battery.c                | 36 ++++++++++++--
 src/battery/tests/test_batterylib.c  |  8 +--
 src/battery/tests/test_dev_battery.c | 73 +++++++++++++++++++++++-----
 3 files changed, 99 insertions(+), 18 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/battery/battery.c b/src/battery/battery.c
index c9ceb62..ceab19c 100644
--- a/src/battery/battery.c
+++ b/src/battery/battery.c
@@ -252,7 +252,18 @@ int battery_temperature(int index)
 		return -1;
 	}
 
-	return (int)temp;
+	/*
+	 * The power_supply class exports temp in tenths of a degree Celsius,
+	 * so 293 is 29.3 degrees. Everything that consumes this treats it as
+	 * whole degrees: batteryd publishes it as "temperature_C", and the
+	 * CTIA limits a few lines up in this file - 0, 57 and 60 - are plainly
+	 * degrees. Left unconverted, an ordinary 30 degree battery read as 300
+	 * and sat permanently above every one of them.
+	 *
+	 * Rounded rather than truncated so that a battery below freezing does
+	 * not come out warmer than it is: -5.5 degrees is -6, not -5.
+	 */
+	return (int)((temp < 0) ? (temp / 10.0 - 0.5) : (temp / 10.0 + 0.5));
 }
 
 /**
@@ -271,7 +282,22 @@ int battery_voltage(int index)
 		return -1;
 	}
 
-	return voltage;
+	/*
+	 * voltage_now is in microvolts. batteryd publishes this as
+	 * "voltage_mV", and battery_coulomb() and battery_full40() below
+	 * already divide their own microamp-hour readings down for exactly the
+	 * same reason - these three simply never got the same treatment, so a
+	 * 3.84 V cell was reported as 3843000 mV.
+	 *
+	 * The old test here asked "should this be in mV or uV? Device returns
+	 * uV but emulator returns mV". The kernel's power_supply ABI settles
+	 * it: voltage_now is microvolts. A driver reporting millivolts is out
+	 * of spec, and is a bug in that driver rather than something to guess
+	 * at here - magnitude cannot be used to tell them apart for current or
+	 * temperature, so guessing for one of the three and not the others
+	 * would be worse than being consistent.
+	 */
+	return voltage / 1000;
 }
 
 /**
@@ -302,7 +328,11 @@ int battery_current(int index)
 		return -1;
 	}
 
-	return (int)current;
+	/* Microamps, for the same reason as the voltage above: batteryd
+	 * publishes it as "current_mA". Signed, so this truncates toward zero
+	 * on both sides, which is what dropping sub-milliamp precision should
+	 * do. */
+	return (int)(current / 1000.0);
 }
 
 /**
diff --git a/src/battery/tests/test_batterylib.c b/src/battery/tests/test_batterylib.c
index b06e9b7..6e5cf3c 100644
